ML_PAGE("l4-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l4\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">LECTURE 4 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Lecture 4 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 50 multiple-choice questions and 7 coding questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with answers hidden until you reveal them.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>50</strong><span>MCQs in the notes</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>7</strong><span>Coding questions</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Difficult-mode questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l4\" data-quiz=\"4\">Start Lecture 4 simulator</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l4\" id=\"l4p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">30 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"Lecture 4 — MCQ Exam Preparation\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s0\" title=\"Lecture 4 — MCQ Exam Preparation\">Overview</button></li><li data-full=\"21. What you should know instantly\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s1\" title=\"21. What you should know instantly\"><span class=\"toc-no\">21</span>Key facts table</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A — TEXT</li><li data-full=\"22. Text preprocessing\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s3\" title=\"22. Text preprocessing\"><span class=\"toc-no\">22</span>Text preprocessing</button></li><li data-full=\"23. Bag of Words\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s4\" title=\"23. Bag of Words\"><span class=\"toc-no\">23</span>Bag of Words</button></li><li data-full=\"24. N-grams\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s5\" title=\"24. N-grams\"><span class=\"toc-no\">24</span>N-grams</button></li><li data-full=\"25. TF-IDF\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s6\" title=\"25. TF-IDF\"><span class=\"toc-no\">25</span>TF-IDF</button></li><li data-full=\"26. Cosine similarity\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s7\" title=\"26. Cosine similarity\"><span class=\"toc-no\">26</span>Cosine similarity</button></li><li data-full=\"27. Document clustering\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s8\" title=\"27. Document clustering\"><span class=\"toc-no\">27</span>Document clustering</button></li><li data-full=\"28. Topic modelling / LDA\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s9\" title=\"28. Topic modelling / LDA\"><span class=\"toc-no\">28</span>Topic modeling (LDA)</button></li><li data-full=\"29. Word embeddings\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s10\" title=\"29. Word embeddings\"><span class=\"toc-no\">29</span>Word embeddings</button></li><li data-full=\"30. CBOW vs Skip-gram\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s11\" title=\"30. CBOW vs Skip-gram\"><span class=\"toc-no\">30</span>CBOW vs skip-gram</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B — TEMPORAL FEATURES</li><li data-full=\"31. Extracting dates\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s13\" title=\"31. Extracting dates\"><span class=\"toc-no\">31</span>Date parts</button></li><li data-full=\"32. Time-of-day binning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s14\" title=\"32. Time-of-day binning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">32</span>Time-of-day bins</button></li><li data-full=\"33. Time zones\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s15\" title=\"33. Time zones\"><span class=\"toc-no\">33</span>Time zones</button></li><li data-full=\"34. Epoch\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s16\" title=\"34. Epoch\"><span class=\"toc-no\">34</span>Epoch time</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART C — IMAGE FEATURES</li><li data-full=\"35. RGB representation\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s18\" title=\"35. RGB representation\"><span class=\"toc-no\">35</span>RGB channels</button></li><li data-full=\"36. Grayscale\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s19\" title=\"36. Grayscale\"><span class=\"toc-no\">36</span>Grayscale</button></li><li data-full=\"37. Flattening\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s20\" title=\"37. Flattening\"><span class=\"toc-no\">37</span>Flattening pixels</button></li><li data-full=\"38. Intensity histograms\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s21\" title=\"38. Intensity histograms\"><span class=\"toc-no\">38</span>Intensity histograms</button></li><li data-full=\"39. Statistical image features\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s22\" title=\"39. Statistical image features\"><span class=\"toc-no\">39</span>Image statistics</button></li><li data-full=\"40. Canny edge detection\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s23\" title=\"40. Canny edge detection\"><span class=\"toc-no\">40</span>Canny edges</button></li><li data-full=\"41. HOG\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s24\" title=\"41. HOG\"><span class=\"toc-no\">41</span>HOG</button></li><li data-full=\"42. SURF\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s25\" title=\"42. SURF\"><span class=\"toc-no\">42</span>SURF</button></li><li data-full=\"43. Visual Bag of Words\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s26\" title=\"43. Visual Bag of Words\"><span class=\"toc-no\">43</span>Visual Bag of Words</button></li><li data-full=\"44. CNNs\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s27\" title=\"44. CNNs\"><span class=\"toc-no\">44</span>CNNs</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART D — INTEGRATION, CODING &amp; REVIEW</li><li data-full=\"45. Lecture 4 coding questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s29\" title=\"45. Lecture 4 coding questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">45</span>Coding questions</button></li><li data-full=\"46. Integration / pipeline MCQs\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s30\" title=\"46. Integration / pipeline MCQs\"><span class=\"toc-no\">46</span>Pipeline order</button></li><li data-full=\"47. Lecture 4 answer key\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s31\" title=\"47. Lecture 4 answer key\"><span class=\"toc-no\">47</span>Answer key</button></li><li data-full=\"48. The distinctions I would memorize hardest for Lecture 4\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s32\" title=\"48. The distinctions I would memorize hardest for Lecture 4\"><span class=\"toc-no\">48</span>Key distinctions</button></li><li data-full=\"49. Final rapid-fire test for both lectures\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s33\" title=\"49. Final rapid-fire test for both lectures\"><span class=\"toc-no\">49</span>Rapid-fire review</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l4\" open><summary><div><strong>Lecture 4 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Know-cold tables, traps, practice questions and coding questions — section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>30 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s0\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Lecture 4 — MCQ Exam Preparation</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Feature Engineering: Text, Temporal &amp; Image Data</h4>\n<p>Lecture 4 is conceptually different.</p>\n<p>Lecture 3 asked:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How do we transform numbers and categories?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Lecture 4 asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How do we convert complicated things like sentences, timestamps and images into numerical representations?</strong></p>\n</blockquote></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">21. What you should know instantly</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>Immediate meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Tokenization</td>\n<td>Split text into tokens/words</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lowercasing</td>\n<td>Normalize capitalization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stopword removal</td>\n<td>Remove frequent low-information words</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stemming</td>\n<td>Heuristic reduction to root</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lemmatization</td>\n<td>Linguistically informed canonical form</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bag of Words</td>\n<td>Word-count vectors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>N-grams</td>\n<td>Contiguous sequences of $n$ words</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TF</td>\n<td>Frequency in current document</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IDF</td>\n<td>Rarity across documents</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TF-IDF</td>\n<td>TF × IDF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cosine similarity</td>\n<td>Compare vector direction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cosine ≈ 1</td>\n<td>Very similar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cosine ≈ 0</td>\n<td>Weak/no similarity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Document clustering</td>\n<td>Group similar texts without labels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LDA</td>\n<td>Discover latent topics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Embeddings</td>\n<td>Dense semantic word vectors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CBOW</td>\n<td>Context → word</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skip-gram</td>\n<td>Word → context</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Date extraction</td>\n<td>Year/month/day/etc.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time binning</td>\n<td>Hour → Morning/Afternoon/etc.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>UTC normalization</td>\n<td>Put time zones on same reference</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Epoch</td>\n<td>Seconds since 1 Jan 1970 UTC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RGB</td>\n<td>Image represented by R/G/B channels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grayscale</td>\n<td>One intensity channel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flattening</td>\n<td>Matrix → 1D vector</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Histogram</td>\n<td>Intensity-frequency representation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Canny</td>\n<td>Edge detection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HOG</td>\n<td>Edge-orientation descriptor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SURF</td>\n<td>Local feature descriptors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VBOW</td>\n<td>Visual descriptors → visual word counts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CNN</td>\n<td>Automated feature learning</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l4p-d2\">PART A — TEXT</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">22. Text preprocessing</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_text_pipeline\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Text preprocessing pipeline</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Type any sentence and watch each preprocessing step.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture's pipeline includes tokenization, lowercasing, removing special characters, stopwords and stemming/lemmatization.</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>What does tokenization do?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Scales numbers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Splits text into units such as words</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Detects image edges</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Calculates cosine similarity</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>Why lowercase text?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> So <code>\"Machine\"</code> and <code>\"machine\"</code> can be treated consistently</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> To remove every noun</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> To scale numbers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> To create timestamps</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>Which is a stopword-like term?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> compressor_failure</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> bearing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> the</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> turbine</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>Stemming and lemmatization both broadly attempt to:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Transform related word forms toward a common form</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Turn words into timestamps</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Detect outliers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Create image pixels</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">23. Bag of Words</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>Bag of Words primarily represents text using:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RGB values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Word occurrence counts</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Time differences</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Category hashing only</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<p>Suppose vocabulary order is:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">cat, dog, sky, blue\n</code></pre>\n<p>Document:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;blue sky&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>Which vector matches?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>[1,1,0,0]</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>[0,0,1,1]</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>[0,1,0,1]</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>[1,0,1,0]</code></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture defines BoW as a term-frequency vector over the corpus vocabulary.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>What is a major limitation of Bag of Words?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It cannot represent numbers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It ignores word positions, syntax and semantic meaning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It requires image data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It cannot count repeated words</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">24. N-grams</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Given:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">machine learning model\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>Which are the bigrams?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>machine</code>, <code>learning</code>, <code>model</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>machine learning</code>, <code>learning model</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>machine learning model</code> only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>machine model</code></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>Why can bigrams help with:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">not good\n</code></pre></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> They can preserve the phrase as a joint feature</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> They remove the word <code>not</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They convert text into images</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They make every word identical</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">25. TF-IDF</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_tfidf\"><div class=\"vis-title\">BoW, TF-IDF and cosine similarity</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Edit the documents; Bag-of-Words counts, TF-IDF weights and cosine similarities update live.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The intuition:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{TF} =\n\\text{importance/frequency within this document}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{IDF} =\n\\text{rarity across all documents}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture's core idea is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>common-everywhere terms get penalized; distinctive terms get greater weight.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>A word appears in every document.</p>\n<p>Its IDF should generally be:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> High</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Low</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Infinite</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Random</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>A rare term appears in only a few documents.</p>\n<p>Its IDF tends to be:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Higher</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Zero</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Always negative</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Equal to term frequency</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>The lecture gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{TFIDF}=\\text{TF}\\times \\text{IDF}$$</div>\n<p>True or False?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> True</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> False</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">26. Cosine similarity</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_cosine\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Cosine similarity</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Cosine similarity measures the angle between document vectors, not their length.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\cos(\\theta)\n=\n\\frac{A\\cdot B}\n{\\|A\\|\\|B\\|}$$</div>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>If cosine similarity is close to:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1$$</div>\n<p>the documents are:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Usually very similar in vector direction</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Completely unrelated</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Duplicates by definition</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Missing data</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>If cosine similarity is near:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0$$</div>\n<p>the documents are:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Highly aligned</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Relatively dissimilar</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Always identical</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Ordinal</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">27. Document clustering</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>A set of unlabeled documents is converted to TF-IDF vectors and automatically grouped into similar topics.</p>\n<p>This is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Regression</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Unsupervised clustering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Supervised classification</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Reinforcement learning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture demonstrates weather documents clustering separately from animal documents.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">28. Topic modelling / LDA</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>What does LDA attempt to discover?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Missing values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Latent topics/themes in documents</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RGB channels</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Numeric outliers</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>In topic modelling, a document may contain:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Exactly one word</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A mixture of multiple topics</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Only one mandatory topic</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> No numeric representation</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">29. Word embeddings</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>Which best describes word embeddings?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sparse one-hot vectors only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Dense numerical vectors where semantically related words can be nearby</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Timestamps for words</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Image pixels</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>Why might vectors for <code>\"king\"</code> and <code>\"queen\"</code> be closer than <code>\"king\"</code> and <code>\"car\"</code>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> They occur in more similar linguistic contexts</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> They have the same spelling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They always have the same length</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They are one-hot encoded</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">30. CBOW vs Skip-gram</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>CBOW roughly predicts:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Context from target word</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Target word from context</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RGB from grayscale</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Topic from timestamp</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>Skip-gram roughly predicts:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Context from target word</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Target word from context</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Income from age</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Categories from quantiles</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The lecture explicitly contrasts CBOW and Skip-gram this way.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l4p-d12\">PART B — TEMPORAL FEATURES</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">31. Extracting dates</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_timestamp\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Timestamp feature extraction</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · One timestamp becomes many features — and the same instant looks different in each time zone.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">2026-09-23 14:35:22\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can derive:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">year\nmonth\nday\nquarter\nday_of_week\nhour\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture explicitly lists these kinds of features.</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>Extracting <code>month</code> from a timestamp is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Feature engineering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Data deletion</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Topic modelling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Image segmentation</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>Why can extracted time components be better than the raw timestamp?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> They can expose patterns such as weekend, season or time-of-day</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Models cannot process any numeric values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They always reduce rows</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They remove categories</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">32. Time-of-day binning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">14:35\n</code></pre>\n<p>becomes:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Afternoon\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>This combines:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Time extraction + binning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> TF-IDF + hashing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> CNN + clustering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Dummy coding + stemming</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">33. Time zones</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>Malaysia:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">14:00 UTC+8\n</code></pre>\n<p>London:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">06:00 UTC+0\n</code></pre>\n<p>These may represent:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Completely different days only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The same moment in time</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Missing data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Image coordinates</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>Why normalize timestamps to UTC?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> To make global timestamps share a consistent temporal reference</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> To perform one-hot encoding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> To eliminate all seasons</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> To create TF-IDF vectors</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">34. Epoch</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>Unix Epoch time represents:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Days since year 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Seconds since 1 January 1970 UTC</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Months since 1900</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Hours since model training</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>Why can epoch/ordinal representations be useful?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Easier elapsed-time calculations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> They automatically classify observations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They remove images</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They eliminate the need for timestamps</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l4p-d17\">PART C — IMAGE FEATURES</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">35. RGB representation</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>An RGB image generally has shape:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$(m,n,c)$$</div>\n<p>where $c$ usually equals:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 2</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 3</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 255</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Red, Green and Blue channels.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>In:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$(m,n,3)$$</div>\n<p>$m$ and $n$ represent:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Model and noise</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Image rows/height and columns/width</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Month and number</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Mean and normalization</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">36. Grayscale</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_rgb3d\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Images as matrices in 3D</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>3D · An 8×8 image as RGB channel matrices, then grayscale, then a flattened vector.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture gives approximately:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Y=\n0.2125R+\n0.7154G+\n0.0721B$$</div>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q31</span><div><p>Converting RGB to grayscale changes approximately:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$(m,n,3)$$</div>\n<p>to:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <div class=\"math-display\">$$(m,n)$$</div></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <div class=\"math-display\">$$(3,m,n,3)$$</div></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <div class=\"math-display\">$$1$$</div></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <div class=\"math-display\">$$(m^2,n^2)$$</div></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q32</span><div><p>Why might grayscale be useful?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Reduces dimensionality while preserving intensity information</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Adds more colour channels</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Converts images into text</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Creates categories automatically</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">37. Flattening</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>A matrix:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">0.2 0.5\n0.7 0.9\n</code></pre>\n<p>can become:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[0.2, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9]\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q33</span><div><p>This is called:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Flattening</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Tokenization</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Binning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Lemmatization</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q34</span><div><p>What is the main drawback?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Spatial neighbour relationships are largely lost</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Pixel values disappear</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Image size doubles</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It creates extra colour channels</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">38. Intensity histograms</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q35</span><div><p>An image histogram can represent:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Number of pixels falling into different intensity ranges</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Word frequencies</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Time zones</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Categorical rankings</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q36</span><div><p>A mostly bright image is likely to have more pixel counts:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> At higher intensity values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Only at zero</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> At negative intensity</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> At random text labels</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">39. Statistical image features</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q37</span><div><p>Which can summarize image intensity distributions?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Mean, variance, skewness, kurtosis</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Only SQL queries</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> TF-IDF only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Generation labels</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">40. Canny edge detection</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q38</span><div><p>What does edge detection primarily seek?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sharp changes in intensity/brightness</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Missing image labels</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Word counts</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Time zones</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q39</span><div><p>Which algorithm is specifically mentioned in the lecture?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Canny</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Apriori</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> PageRank</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> ARIMA</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">41. HOG</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q40</span><div><p>HOG stands for:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Histogram of Oriented Gradients</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Hierarchy of Global Groups</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> High Order Gaussian</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Hash of Gradients</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q41</span><div><p>HOG primarily captures:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Edge directions/orientations and local shape information</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Word semantics</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> SQL structure</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Datetime offsets</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s25\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">42. SURF</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q42</span><div><p>SURF is mainly associated with:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Localized feature extraction around interesting image points</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Text stemming</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Time binning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Categorical encoding</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q43</span><div><p>SURF stands for:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Standard Unified Regression Features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Speeded Up Robust Features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Structured Universal Ranking Formula</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Scaled Unsupervised Random Forest</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">43. Visual Bag of Words</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q44</span><div><p>Visual Bag of Words is inspired by:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Text Bag of Words</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Min-Max scaling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Regression</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Epoch timestamps</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q45</span><div><p>The basic VBOW flow is approximately:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Image → Local descriptors → Clusters → Visual words → Count vector\"><span>Image</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Local descriptors</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Clusters</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Visual words</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Count vector</span></div>\n<p>True or False?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> True</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> False</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The lecture describes clustering local descriptors and treating clusters as visual words.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s27\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">44. CNNs</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q46</span><div><p>What major change do CNNs bring compared with traditional image-feature engineering?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> CNNs can learn useful feature representations automatically</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> CNNs require all features to be manually designed</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> CNNs convert images to SQL</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> CNNs eliminate numerical input</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q47</span><div><p>Early CNN layers commonly learn:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Edges and lines</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Entire high-level objects immediately</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Time zones</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Document topics</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q48</span><div><p>Deeper CNN layers generally learn:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Increasingly complex patterns/concepts</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Only pixel brightness</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Missing-value indicators</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Stopwords</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l4p-d28\">PART D — INTEGRATION, CODING &amp; REVIEW</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s29\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">45. Lecture 4 coding questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Coding Q1 — Bag of Words</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer\n\ncv = CountVectorizer()\nX = cv.fit_transform(documents)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is <code>X</code>?</p>\n<p>A. Word-count feature representation<br />\nB. Timestamp matrix<br />\nC. Image matrix<br />\nD. Scaling parameters</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: A</strong></p></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q2 — N-grams</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">CountVectorizer(ngram_range=(1, 2))\n</code></pre>\n<p>What does this allow?</p>\n<p>A. Only single words<br />\nB. Unigrams and bigrams<br />\nC. Bigrams and trigrams only<br />\nD. Image patches</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: B</strong></p></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q3 — TF-IDF</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer\n\ntfidf = TfidfVectorizer()\nX = tfidf.fit_transform(documents)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is being produced?</p>\n<p>A. Weighted text vectors<br />\nB. RGB images<br />\nC. Time intervals<br />\nD. One-hot countries</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: A</strong></p></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q4 — cosine similarity</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\n\nsimilarity = cosine_similarity(X)\n</code></pre>\n<p>If two document vectors are nearly identical in direction, their similarity approaches:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1$$</div>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q5 — datetime features</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;month&quot;] = df[&quot;time&quot;].dt.month\ndf[&quot;hour&quot;] = df[&quot;time&quot;].dt.hour\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is this?</p>\n<p>A. Temporal feature engineering<br />\nB. Text tokenization<br />\nC. Image normalization<br />\nD. Hashing</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: A</strong></p></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q6 — time binning</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;time_of_day&quot;] = pd.cut(\n    df[&quot;hour&quot;],\n    bins=[0, 6, 12, 18, 24],\n    labels=[&quot;Night&quot;, &quot;Morning&quot;, &quot;Afternoon&quot;, &quot;Evening&quot;]\n)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What technique is being combined?</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Temporal extraction + binning.</p></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q7 — image flattening</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">X = image.flatten()\n</code></pre>\n<p>What happens?</p>\n<p>A. 2D/3D image data becomes a 1D vector<br />\nB. Text becomes lower case<br />\nC. RGB becomes TF-IDF<br />\nD. Image becomes categorical</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: A</strong></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s30\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">46. Integration / pipeline MCQs</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture closes with the correct order:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Train/Test Split → Fit Transformers on Train → Transform Train → Transform Test → Train Model → Evaluate\"><span>Train/Test Split</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Fit Transformers on Train</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Transform Train</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Transform Test</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Train Model</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Evaluate</span></div>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q49</span><div><p>Why split train/test <strong>before</strong> fitting TF-IDF, quantile binning, scalers or encoders?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> To avoid test information leaking into preprocessing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> To increase image resolution</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> To convert categories to text</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> To reduce rows automatically</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q50</span><div><p>Which is wrong?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Fit TF-IDF vocabulary on train, transform train/test</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Fit encoder on train, reuse on test</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Refit preprocessing separately on test</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Split before learning preprocessing parameters</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s31\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">47. Lecture 4 answer key</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the answer key</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Q</th>\n<th class=\"ta-c\">Answer</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Q</th>\n<th class=\"ta-c\">Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">1</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">26</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">2</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">27</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">3</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">28</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">4</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">29</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">5</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">30</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">6</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">31</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">7</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">32</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">8</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">33</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">9</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">34</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">10</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">35</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">11</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">36</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">12</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">True</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">37</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">13</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">38</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">14</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">39</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">15</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">40</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">16</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">41</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">17</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">42</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">18</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">43</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">19</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">44</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">20</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">45</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">True</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">21</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">46</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">22</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">47</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">23</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">48</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">24</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">49</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">25</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">50</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr /></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s32\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">48. The distinctions I would memorize hardest for Lecture 4</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>For <strong>text</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{BoW}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Counts}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nN\\text{-}\\text{grams}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Some local word order}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{TFIDF}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Counts weighted by distinctiveness}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Embeddings}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Semantic vectors}\n}$$</div>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{CBOW}:\\ \\text{Context}\\rightarrow \\text{Word}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{SkipGram}:\\ \\text{Word}\\rightarrow \\text{Context}$$</div>\n<p>For <strong>temporal</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Timestamp}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Year/Month/Day/Hour/Weekday/etc}.\n}$$</div>\n<p>and don't forget:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Normalize global timezones to}\\ a\\ \\text{common reference}}$$</div>\n<p>For <strong>images</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RGB}=(\\text{height},\\text{width},3)}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Pixels}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Grayscale/Histogram/Edges/HOG/SURF}\n}$$</div>\n<p>or modern approach:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Pixels → CNN → Automatically learned features\"><span>Pixels</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>CNN</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Automatically learned features</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s33\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">49. Final rapid-fire test for both lectures</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Answer each in about <strong>2–3 seconds</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Only care whether count &gt; 0</td>\n<td>Binarization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>87.638294 where decimals are noise</td>\n<td>Rounding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Temperature × Humidity</td>\n<td>Interaction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Age → 30–39</td>\n<td>Binning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Same numerical range widths</td>\n<td>Fixed-width</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Same approximate observation counts</td>\n<td>Quantile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strong right-skewed salary</td>\n<td>Log transform</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flexible power transform</td>\n<td>Box-Cox</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Colour</td>\n<td>Nominal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Small &lt; Medium &lt; Large</td>\n<td>Ordinal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nominal text → binary columns</td>\n<td>One-hot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$m$ categories → $m-1$ columns</td>\n<td>Dummy coding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thousands of categories</td>\n<td>Feature hashing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Split sentence into words</td>\n<td>Tokenization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Count words</td>\n<td>BoW</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>\"not good\"</code> as one feature</td>\n<td>Bigram</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Penalize globally common terms</td>\n<td>TF-IDF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Similarity ≈ 1</td>\n<td>Very similar vectors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Discover topics</td>\n<td>LDA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Semantic numerical word representations</td>\n<td>Embeddings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Context → target word</td>\n<td>CBOW</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Target word → context</td>\n<td>Skip-gram</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Timestamp → month/hour</td>\n<td>Temporal feature extraction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Malaysia and London timestamps</td>\n<td>Normalize UTC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seconds since 1970</td>\n<td>Epoch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Image height × width × 3</td>\n<td>RGB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RGB → one intensity matrix</td>\n<td>Grayscale</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Detect sharp brightness changes</td>\n<td>Canny</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Edge orientation descriptor</td>\n<td>HOG</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Local keypoint descriptor</td>\n<td>SURF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cluster local descriptors into visual words</td>\n<td>VBOW</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Automatically learn image features</td>\n<td>CNN</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>If you can do that table without hesitation, you have the high-yield conceptual material from <strong>Lectures 3 and 4</strong> in good shape.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l4-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Difficult mode — 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry, coding, hackathon, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the lecture&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"4\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Lecture 4 difficult mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"4\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which order matches the lecture's text-preprocessing pipeline?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Tokenise → lowercase → remove special characters → remove stopwords → stem / lemmatise</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Stem → tokenise → lowercase → remove stopwords → remove special characters</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Remove stopwords → tokenise → stem → lowercase → remove special characters</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Lowercase → stem → tokenise → remove stopwords → TF-IDF</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The slides list <strong>tokenisation → lowercasing → special characters → stopwords → stemming / lemmatisation</strong>. B stems before splitting into words. C removes stopwords before there are tokens. D mixes in feature extraction (TF-IDF), which comes <em>after</em> cleaning.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>In Word2Vec, what does the CBOW architecture predict?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The surrounding context words from the target word.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The document's topic mixture.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The TF-IDF weight of each word.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The target word from its surrounding context words.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><strong>CBOW: context → target.</strong> A describes Skip-gram (target → context). B is LDA. C is unrelated.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which grayscale formula does the lecture use?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $Y = (R + G + B)/3$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $Y = 0.2125R + 0.7154G + 0.0721B$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $Y = 0.7154R + 0.2125G + 0.0721B$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $Y = \\max(R, G, B)$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Green gets the largest weight (≈ 72%) because human eyes are most sensitive to it. C swaps the red and green weights. A is a plain average. D is the \"value\" channel of HSV, not luminance.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Using the lecture's $\\text{IDF}(t) = \\log(N/\\text{df}(t))$, why does a word that appears in <strong>every</strong> document end up with zero weight?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because stopwords are always deleted first.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because TF is zero for very common words.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because $N/\\text{df} = 1$ and $\\log 1 = 0$: a word found everywhere cannot distinguish one document from another.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because IDF is only defined for rare words.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>IDF measures how <strong>distinctive</strong> a term is across the corpus. A term in all N documents has no discriminating power, so its weight vanishes, however frequent it is within a document. A confuses cleaning with weighting. B is false — TF can be high. D is false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why does the lecture prefer cosine similarity to raw Euclidean distance for comparing documents?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Cosine compares the <strong>direction</strong> of count vectors, so a long document and a short one with the same word proportions count as similar.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Cosine is faster to compute.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Euclidean distance cannot be computed on sparse vectors.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Cosine ignores which words appear.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>\"dog cat animal\" and \"dog dog cat cat animal animal\" point the <strong>same way</strong>, so cosine is 1, but they are far apart in Euclidean terms because one is twice as long. The difference is about meaning, not speed (B). C and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Logs come from servers in Kuala Lumpur (UTC+8) and London (UTC+0). Why convert to UTC <strong>before</strong> extracting <code>hour</code>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> UTC timestamps use less memory.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The same instant shows a different local hour on each server; extracting local hours would create fake 8-hour gaps and mismatched patterns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> pandas cannot extract hours from local times.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> UTC removes weekends.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>14:00 in KL and 06:00 in London can be the <strong>same moment</strong>. Features derived from unaligned clocks misrepresent behaviour — the lecture names this as one of its five common mistakes. A, C and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p><code>CountVectorizer(ngram_range=(1, 2))</code> is applied to the single document <code>\"not good at all\"</code>, with no stopword removal. How many features does it produce?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 4</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 6</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 10</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 7</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>4 unigrams (not, good, at, all) + 3 bigrams (not good, good at, at all) = <strong>7</strong>. A counts unigrams only. B and C miscount the bigrams. Keeping \"not good\" as one feature is the lecture's reason for using n-grams.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>What is the cosine similarity between $A = [1, 1, 0]$ and $B = [1, 0, 1]$?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.71</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$A \\cdot B = 1$ and $\\|A\\| = \\|B\\| = \\sqrt{2}$, so $\\cos\\theta = 1/2 = 0.5$: one shared term out of two each. B would need no shared terms; C identical directions. D ($1/\\sqrt{2}$) forgets to divide by the second vector's norm.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A 28 × 28 RGB image is flattened directly. If it is first converted to grayscale and then flattened, how many features are there in each case?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 784 and 2,352</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 2,352 and 784</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 2,352 and 2,352</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 84 and 28</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>RGB: $28 \\times 28 \\times 3 = 2{,}352$. Grayscale reduces $(m, n, 3)$ to $(m, n)$, giving $28 \\times 28 = 784$, so grayscale cuts the dimension by 3×. A swaps the two. D sums rows instead of multiplying.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>\"dog bites man\" and \"man bites dog\" produce <strong>identical</strong> Bag-of-Words vectors. Which change lets a model tell them apart?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> TF-IDF weighting.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Lowercasing.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Adding bigrams such as \"dog bites\" and \"bites man\".</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Removing stopwords.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>BoW throws away word order. Both sentences have the same counts, so any <strong>reweighting</strong> of those counts (A) stays identical. Bigrams keep local order: \"dog bites\" occurs in one sentence, \"bites dog\" in the other. B and D don't add order information.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>An algorithm returns, for each news article, something like <code>{sports: 0.7, politics: 0.3}</code>. Which method most likely produced it, and why?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> LDA topic modelling — each document is a mixture of topics.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> k-means document clustering — each document gets one cluster label.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Bag of Words — it counts words.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Word2Vec — it outputs word vectors.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Proportions across several topics are the signature of <strong>topic modelling</strong>: documents are mixtures. Clustering (B) assigns each document to one group. C and D produce feature vectors, not topic mixtures.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Image P is a photo of a cat. Image Q contains exactly the same pixel values, randomly shuffled. Which features still <strong>differ</strong> between them?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The intensity histogram.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Mean, variance, skewness and kurtosis.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Edge maps and HOG descriptors.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> None — all features are identical.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Histograms and summary statistics depend only on <strong>which</strong> values occur, not <strong>where</strong>, so A and B are identical for P and Q. Edges and HOG measure intensity changes between neighbouring pixels: P has contours, Q is noise. That is the lecture's point that histograms lose spatial information.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A legal search engine shows the matched normalised terms to lawyers, so they must be real words (\"studies\" → \"study\", never \"studi\"). Which normalisation is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Stemming, because it is faster.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Lemmatisation, because it uses vocabulary and morphology to return valid dictionary forms.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> No normalisation, because legal text is precise.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Remove all words longer than 6 letters.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture contrasts <strong>heuristic</strong> stemming, which may produce non-words, with <strong>linguistic</strong> lemmatisation, which returns canonical forms. The requirement for real words decides it. A trades correctness for speed. C loses the matches between word forms. D is nonsense.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>For a food-delivery demand model, which timestamp representation is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Raw epoch seconds only.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The timestamp as a label-encoded string.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Nothing — timestamps leak information.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>hour</code>, <code>day_of_week</code>, <code>is_weekend</code> and a time-of-day bin from UTC-aligned times, plus elapsed-time features where needed.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Demand follows <strong>daily and weekly cycles</strong>. Extracted parts expose them directly — the lecture's food-delivery example. A is monotonically increasing and hides the cycles. B is effectively a unique ID per row. C is false — timestamps are only a problem when they reveal the future.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>You have 500 labelled images and limited compute. Which approach is most reasonable <strong>within the lecture's toolkit</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Extract HOG or edge features and train a classical classifier.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Train a deep CNN from scratch, because CNNs always beat hand-crafted features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Flatten raw pixels and use k-NN, because it needs no training.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Use only intensity histograms, because they are fastest.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>CNNs learn features automatically, but from-scratch training on 500 images is very likely to <strong>overfit</strong> (Lecture 1). Hand-crafted descriptors encode useful shape priors cheaply. C suffers from high dimensionality and the loss of spatial structure. D throws away all shape information.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Design a spam-filter text representation where \"not free\" must differ from \"free\", and generic words currently dominate.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Remove all stopwords, including \"not\"; use unigram counts.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Lowercase and tokenise; keep negations; use unigrams + bigrams; weight with TF-IDF fitted on the training emails only.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Stem aggressively and use raw counts.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Use one-hot document IDs.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Bigrams keep \"not free\"; keeping negations stops cleaning from deleting the signal; <strong>TF-IDF</strong> fixes the dominance of generic words; and fitting on train only avoids leakage. A deletes the negation the task depends on. C leaves both problems unsolved. D has no textual content.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Equipment failure data has <code>machine_type</code>, <code>temperature</code> (noisy decimals), <code>maintenance_note</code> (text), <code>timestamp</code> (several sites) and an inspection image. Which design is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Label-encode every column, then train a single model.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Concatenate everything into one string and use TF-IDF.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Split first; then, fitted on train only: one-hot <code>machine_type</code>; round or bin <code>temperature</code>; TF-IDF the notes; convert timestamps to UTC and extract parts; image → edges/HOG or CNN features; combine and train.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Use only the image, because CNNs learn everything.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>That's the lecture's <strong>\"one machine, five representations\"</strong> with the pipeline rule: split first, fit on train. A imposes a false order on machine types and ignores the text and image structure. B destroys numeric meaning. D discards cheap, highly informative structured signals.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Design a \"find similar documents\" feature for 50,000 support tickets.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Fit TF-IDF on the corpus, represent the query with the same vectoriser, rank tickets by cosine similarity, and return the top k.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Rank by Euclidean distance between raw word-count vectors.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Cluster tickets with k-means and return a random ticket from the query's cluster.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Compare ticket lengths.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>TF-IDF down-weights generic words, and cosine ignores document length — the lecture's exact combination for <strong>document similarity</strong>. B favours tickets of similar length and lets common words dominate. C gives coarse, unranked results. D ignores content.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>Levy &amp; Goldberg (2014) showed that skip-gram with negative sampling (SGNS), with $k$ negative samples, implicitly factorises which matrix?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The raw word–document count matrix.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The TF-IDF matrix.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The word–context PMI matrix shifted by $\\log k$: $\\text{PMI}(w, c) - \\log k$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The identity matrix.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>At the optimum, SGNS's dot products satisfy $\\vec w \\cdot \\vec c = \\text{PMI}(w, c) - \\log k$. So Word2Vec embeddings are a low-rank factorisation of co-occurrence statistics. That links the lecture's \"counts → meaning\" step formally: embeddings compress <em>context</em> counts, while BoW uses <em>document</em> counts.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Coding</span></div><p>scikit-learn's <code>TfidfVectorizer</code> (default <code>smooth_idf=True</code>) computes $\\text{idf}(t) = \\ln\\frac{1 + n}{1 + \\text{df}(t)} + 1$. With <strong>n = 4</strong> documents and a term in <strong>all 4</strong>, what is its idf?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\ln 4 \\approx 1.386$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Undefined</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 1</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>$\\ln(5/5) + 1 = 0 + 1 = \\mathbf{1}$. Unlike the lecture's plain $\\log(N/\\text{df})$, which gives 0 (A), sklearn adds <strong>+1</strong>, so ubiquitous terms keep a small weight, and the smoothing prevents division by zero. Always check the library's definition: hand-computed answers and sklearn output can differ.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Two document vectors are <strong>L2-normalised</strong> and have cosine similarity 0.6. What is their squared Euclidean distance, and what does that imply?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.4; Euclidean and cosine rankings can disagree.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.8; for unit vectors, $\\|a - b\\|^2 = 2 - 2\\cos\\theta$, so ranking by Euclidean distance equals ranking by cosine.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1.6; Euclidean is always larger.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.36; square the cosine.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>For unit vectors, $\\|a-b\\|^2 = \\|a\\|^2 + \\|b\\|^2 - 2a\\cdot b = 2 - 2(0.6) = 0.8$. Distance is a monotone function of cosine, so after L2 normalisation (sklearn's TF-IDF default) the two give the <strong>same nearest neighbours</strong>. The lecture's \"use cosine\" advice is really about length; normalisation removes length.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Hackathon</span></div><p>Hour of day is encoded as $(\\sin(2\\pi h/24), \\cos(2\\pi h/24))$. What is the Euclidean distance between hour 23 and hour 0, compared with the raw-hour distance?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> About 0.26 (vs 23 for raw hours)</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 23 in both encodings</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 2.0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>On the unit circle, adjacent hours are close: the distance is $2\\sin(\\pi/24) \\approx 0.261$, whereas raw integers put 23 and 0 maximally far apart. C (2.0) is the distance between hours 12 and 0. The lecture bins hour into Morning/Afternoon/Night; cyclical encoding keeps the continuity across midnight instead.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Coding</span></div><p>A HOG descriptor uses a 64 × 128 image, 8 × 8-pixel cells, 2 × 2-cell blocks with a stride of 1 cell, and 9 orientation bins. How long is the feature vector?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1,152</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 3,780</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 4,608</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 3,240</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Cells: 8 × 16. Block positions: (8 − 1) × (16 − 1) = 7 × 15 = 105. Each block holds 2 × 2 cells × 9 bins = 36 values, so 105 × 36 = <strong>3,780</strong> — the classic Dalal–Triggs pedestrian detector. A counts cells × bins without blocks. C uses 128 block positions.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>What is the receptive field of one output unit after: 3 × 3 conv (stride 1) → 2 × 2 max-pool (stride 2) → 3 × 3 conv (stride 1)?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 8 × 8</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 7 × 7</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 6 × 6</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 9 × 9</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Use $r \\mathrel{+}= (k-1)\\cdot j$ and $j \\mathrel{*}= s$. Start: r = 1, j = 1. Conv3: r = 3. Pool2/stride 2: r = 3 + 1 = 4, j = 2. Conv3: r = 4 + 2 × 2 = <strong>8</strong>. B is three stacked 3 × 3 convs with no pooling. This is the mechanism behind \"early layers see edges, deeper layers see shapes\": each layer widens the area a unit sees.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A word-level BoW model has never seen \"unhappiness\". A subword (BPE / WordPiece) tokeniser splits it into \"un\", \"happi\", \"ness\". Why does this help?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It removes the word from the text.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Rare and unseen words share pieces with known words, so the model still gets meaningful features instead of an out-of-vocabulary blank, and the vocabulary stays small.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It turns text into images.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It guarantees correct spelling.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Word-level vocabularies can't represent unseen words. Subwords <strong>compose</strong> them from frequent fragments (\"un-\" negation, \"-ness\" noun). It's a learned middle ground between characters and words, extending the lecture's tokenisation step. A, C and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>An hourly demand series stored in <strong>London local time</strong> shows one missing hour in late March and a doubled hour in late October. What is the cause and the fix?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sensor failure; interpolate both.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Leap seconds; ignore them.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A pandas bug; upgrade the library.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Daylight-saving transitions: one local hour doesn't exist in spring and one repeats in autumn. Store and aggregate in UTC, and convert to local time only for display or local-hour features.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>On the spring change, clocks jump from 01:00 to 02:00, so that hour never happens; in autumn, 01:00–01:59 happens twice. Local wall-clock time isn't a continuous axis. The lecture's \"normalise to UTC\" rule fixes it. A papers over the problem and corrupts the data. B and C are wrong.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>Search engines often prefer <strong>BM25</strong> to plain TF-IDF. Which statement best explains why?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> BM25 saturates term frequency (the 20th \"refund\" adds little over the 5th) and normalises for document length, so long, repetitive documents don't win just by repetition.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> BM25 ignores rare words.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> BM25 needs labelled training data.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> BM25 is TF-IDF without the IDF term.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>BM25 keeps IDF's distinctiveness idea (so D is false) but adds a <strong>saturating</strong> TF curve controlled by $k_1$ and a <strong>length normalisation</strong> controlled by $b$. That fixes the two weaknesses of raw-count TF that follow from the lecture's \"problem with raw word counts\". B and C are false; BM25 is unsupervised.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>Which statement about CNNs and translation is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> CNNs are perfectly translation-invariant at every layer.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Convolution is translation-<strong>equivariant</strong> (shift the input, the feature map shifts); pooling adds only limited local invariance, and striding/aliasing can make outputs change under small shifts.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> CNNs have no relationship to translation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Flattening pixels gives better translation invariance than convolution.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Shared kernels make convolution equivariant. Invariance comes only partly, from pooling and global aggregation, and downsampling breaks exact shift-invariance — a known source of brittleness. It refines the lecture's point that flattening destroys spatial structure (D is backwards).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>You have 500 labelled medical images. Option 1: train a CNN from scratch. Option 2: extract frozen embeddings from a large self-supervised or contrastively pre-trained image model (e.g. CLIP-style) and train a linear classifier (\"linear probe\"). Which assessment is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Option 1 is always better, because task-specific training beats pre-training.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both are equivalent, because 500 images is enough for any method.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Option 2 usually generalises better with few labels, because pre-trained representations encode general visual structure. But you must check for domain shift (natural vs medical images) and validate on held-out patients; fine-tuning or domain-specific pre-training may still be needed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Option 2 needs no validation, because the model is pre-trained.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>This extends the lecture's arc from hand-crafted features (HOG) to <em>learned</em> features (CNNs) and then to <em>transferred</em> representations. With few labels, reusing representations reduces variance (Lecture 1). Domain shift is the key risk, so representativeness still matters. A and B ignore sample-size effects. D ignores evaluation entirely.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A team augmented 1,000 images ×10 with flips and crops, then randomly split the 10,000 into train and test. Validation accuracy is 99%. Which protocol is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Augment, then split — more test images make evaluation more reliable.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Augment only the test set.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Don't augment at all, ever.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Split the original images first (grouped by source image), then augment <strong>only</strong> the training set; evaluate on untouched originals.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Augmented copies of one photo are near-duplicates. Splitting after augmentation puts siblings in both sets — the lecture's duplicate-leakage and split-first rule for images. A causes the inflated 99%. B and C misunderstand augmentation's purpose, which is more training variety.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
